# Feature Structure

## Ownership

- Treat each page as a module. A module owns everything only it uses, including its API calls, and lives in `src/modules/<feature>`.
- Put cross-feature code in `src/shared`. Promote an API resource to `src/shared/api/<resource>` only once a second module needs it (e.g. a search endpoint used by home, listing, and details).
- Do not import from one module into another module unless the imported code is truly shared. Move shared code to `src/shared` first.

## Recommended Module Shape

- `components/` for feature UI.
- Group components by the page/route they belong to inside `components/`. When a module has nested routes (e.g. `/properties` and `/properties/[slug]`), give each route its own subfolder (`components/properties/`, `components/property-details/`) so it is clear which page each component serves. Keep components shared across those routes in a `components/shared/` (or `components/common/`) subfolder.
- For large features, split `components/` into per-section or per-page subfolders instead of a single flat folder, so the structure stays scannable.
- `api/` for endpoints only this module uses, following the resource shape in `api-state-data` (`types.ts`, `server.ts`, `hooks/use-*.ts`).
- `types.ts` for feature-specific types.
- `utils.ts` for feature-specific pure helpers.
- `constants.ts` for repeated static data/options.
- `hooks/` for feature-specific hooks that are not tied to a request.
- `store/` only when feature state must be shared across multiple components and a store is needed.

## Component Boundaries

- Keep components focused on one responsibility.
- Extract large sections, repeated cards, filters, sidebars, and dialogs into named components.
- Keep page-level composition readable: data in, sections out.
- Prefer collocating small helpers with the component until they become reusable.

## Naming

- Files and folders use lowercase kebab-case (e.g. `question-card.tsx`, `components/auth-wizard`).
- React components are PascalCase, and a single-component file's name matches its component (`question-card.tsx` exports `QuestionCard`).
- Hooks start with `use` and live in camelCase files prefixed with `use-` (e.g. `use-questions.ts` exporting `useQuestions`).
- Event handlers use the `handle` prefix (`handleClick`, `handleSubmit`); props that pass them through use the `on` prefix (`onClick`).
- Types and interfaces are PascalCase; prefer explicit names like `QuestionCardProps` over generic ones.
- Exported constant objects/maps are UPPER_SNAKE or descriptive PascalCase (`ROUTES`, `URLs`); local constants stay camelCase.
- Boolean variables read as predicates (`isLoading`, `hasError`, `canSubmit`).
- Constants should use descriptive names and stay near their owner unless shared.
